# Data Science and Multilingual NLP — 16-Week Curriculum

Sep 24, 2026 · @mru

## Objective

In weeks 8–16, build one multilingual system that extracts entities from documents and answers questions over them, with a per-language evaluation for every component.

**Final deliverable:** a GitHub repo with a FastAPI service (`/extract`, `/ask`), a one-command eval suite, and a README containing the architecture diagram, results tables, and one design decision memo.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    D[Documents<br/>4 languages] --> E[Entity extraction<br/>fine-tuned XLM-R vs LLM]
    D --> R[Retrieval<br/>BM25 / bi-encoder]
    R --> RR[Cross-encoder<br/>reranker]
    RR --> G[LLM answer<br/>generation]
    G --> F[Faithfulness +<br/>correctness eval]
    E --> M[Per-language<br/>F1 eval]
```

Every box is a week of work, and every box is an interview topic.

**What it proves in an interview:**

- Classic NLP depth: tokenization, fine-tuning, token classification, cross-lingual transfer.
- Retrieval depth: sparse vs dense, bi-encoder vs cross-encoder, retrieval metrics.
- Eval depth: faithfulness built from scratch, and a judge you validated against human labels.
- Senior judgement: a measured "fine-tuned model vs LLM" decision on cost, latency and accuracy.

It answers both Elsevier questions you missed: the cross-encoder and faithfulness code, and the multilingual extraction design.

## Sequence

Traditional ML and statistics fundamentals come first (weeks 1–7), then the multilingual project (weeks 8–16). One track at a time, with a buffer week built in. Why this order: most UK data scientist interviews test GLMs, segmentation, hypothesis testing and experiment design in the first technical round. Your GenAI work is what makes you stand out, but these topics are what get you through that round, so they come first.

